# The Suspension Tower of Free Z/2-Complexes

Welcome to an interactive tour of a simple machine that builds spheres one
dimension at a time. Start with any symmetric shape, glue on two poles without
joining them, and repeat: the result is a *tower* whose height we can measure
exactly.

## 1. Growing a tower

The central law is disarmingly clean: **each suspension adds exactly one to the
dimension**, so a base of dimension $d$ becomes $S^k$ of dimension $d+k$. Explore
it directly, together with the tower's co-index and (zero) excess:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The staircase below shows the same law across several octahedral bases: every
line has unit slope.

{{visualization:0}}

Under the hood, the dimension is computed by enumerating faces and taking the
largest, and the tower is built by iterating the suspension constructor:

{{algorithm:0}}

{{algorithm:3}}

## 2. Counting the faces of a crystal

The octahedral sphere $\mathrm{Oct}(n)$ has a strikingly clean census of its
top-dimensional faces: they are exactly the $2^{n+1}$ **orthants**, one per sign
vector. Browse them:

